    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Workplace Strategy Consultation", href: "/workplace-strategy" }, { anchor: "Case Studies", href: "/case-studies" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Australian workplace design has shifted fundamentally since 2022. The leading organisations are no longer optimising for density — they're optimising for experience, wellbeing, and the magnetic pull that draws people to choose the office over home. The office that competes with the home office must be meaningfully better in ways that matter to the person sitting in it.</p>

<h2>1. The End of Uniform Open Plan</h2>
<p>The fully open-plan office — rows of benching, no acoustic separation, no variety of settings — is being actively reversed by organisations that implemented it in the 2010s. The evidence that open plan reduces productivity and increases stress has accumulated to the point where no credible workplace designer still advocates for it as the dominant setting. What's replacing it is a palette of settings: open collaboration zones, enclosed focus booths, small group rooms, phone pods, lounge areas, and quiet library-style zones — with the mix determined by how the specific team actually works.</p>

<h2>2. Biophilic Design Moves from Trend to Standard</h2>
<p>Biophilic design — the integration of natural materials, plants, natural light, water features, and organic forms — has moved from aspirational to expected in premium commercial fitouts. The research backing is solid: biophilic environments reduce cortisol, improve cognitive performance, and increase reported wellbeing. Australian clients are specifying timber veneer furniture, stone surfaces, planting walls, and natural textile upholstery as standard elements rather than premium additions.</p>

<h2>3. Acoustic Design as a Primary Specification</h2>
<p>Acoustic comfort has risen to the top of user satisfaction surveys for the last three consecutive years. Organisations are investing in acoustic ceiling panels, wall tiles, privacy pods, and furniture with integrated acoustic screens not as afterthoughts but as core infrastructure. The specification of furniture is increasingly acoustic-first — pod chairs, high-back sofas, and screen-integrated workstations are specified for their acoustic contribution as much as their appearance.</p>

<h2>4. Hybrid Work Drives Neighbourhood Planning</h2>
<p>With attendance patterns now distributed across the week, the office floor plan is being reorganised around team neighbourhoods — dedicated zones for specific teams that create a sense of ownership and belonging despite not having assigned desks. Each neighbourhood has a mix of bench seating, collaboration tables, lounge settings, and enclosed meeting spaces. This approach is replacing the first-come-first-served hot-desking model that was widely rejected by staff.</p>

<h2>5. Wellness Infrastructure Becomes Competitive Differentiator</h2>
<p>Wellness spaces — quiet rooms, meditation rooms, parent rooms, prayer rooms, gym facilities — are being incorporated into commercial fitouts as standard elements rather than luxury additions. The organisations that provide genuine wellbeing infrastructure are retaining and attracting staff more effectively. Ergonomic furniture specification — sit-stand desks, AFRDI Level 6 chairs, monitor arms, anti-fatigue matting — is seen as a basic duty of care rather than a benefit.</p>

<h2>6. Premium Materials Return to Executive Environments</h2>
<p>After a decade of glass and white laminate, premium materials are returning to Australian executive environments. Timber veneer, stone, brushed metal, and leather are being specified for boardrooms, reception areas, and executive suites. The corporate austerity aesthetic of the 2010s is giving way to considered luxury — not ostentatious, but genuinely high quality and deliberately crafted.</p>

<h2>7. Technology Integration Becomes Invisible</h2>
<p>The best-designed offices of 2025 hide their technology rather than display it. Cable management, in-surface power access, wireless charging integration, and clean HDMI connections replace the cable chaos of earlier fitouts. Tables with recessed power modules, chairs with tablet arms and integrated USB ports, and AV systems operated by a single touch rather than a tangle of remotes define the premium end of the market.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design for 2025 and Beyond</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk specialises in premium commercial fitouts that reflect current best practice in workplace design. Request a consultation to discuss your project.</p>
</div>
`,
