    metaDescription: "An honest assessment of activity-based working (ABW) in Australian offices. What works, what fails, and how to implement ABW effectively for your organisation.",
    primaryKeyword: "activity-based working Australia",
    secondaryKeywords: ["ABW office design", "activity based workplace", "hot desking Australia"],
    readTime: "8 min read",
    publishDate: "2025-12-05",
    excerpt: "Activity-based working has been praised, criticised, and widely misunderstood in Australia. The failures are real, but so are the successes. The difference is almost entirely in implementation quality, not the principle itself.",
    tags: ["ABW", "workplace", "trends", "strategy"],
    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Workplace Strategy Consultation", href: "/workplace-strategy" }, { anchor: "Case Studies", href: "/case-studies" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Activity-based working (ABW) has been praised as the future of work, criticised as a cost-cutting measure dressed up in design language, and widely misunderstood across Australian commercial property. The failures are documented and real — organisations that implemented ABW poorly have seen productivity decline, staff satisfaction plummet, and in some high-profile cases, unions intervene. But the successes are equally real. The difference is almost entirely in implementation quality, not in the principle itself.</p>

<h2>What ABW Actually Means</h2>
<p>Activity-based working means that workers choose from a variety of settings based on the task they're performing, rather than returning to an assigned desk. A focused writing task requires a quiet, distraction-free zone. A collaborative brainstorm requires a whiteboard and group seating. A confidential call requires an enclosed pod. A casual conversation can happen in a lounge setting. The office provides all these settings; the worker moves between them as their work demands change throughout the day.</p>

<h2>Why ABW Fails</h2>
<p>ABW fails when it is implemented as a cost-reduction exercise without genuine investment in the settings it requires. The most common failure mode: an organisation reduces desk count by 30% (cost saving), calls the result ABW, but provides no enclosed quiet spaces, no acoustic treatment, no bookable meeting rooms at the required ratio, and no change management support for the cultural transition. The result is staff fighting for the limited enclosed spaces, reverting to their old desks out of habit and territory, and resenting the loss of personal space without any of the claimed benefits.</p>

<h2>The Settings Required for Successful ABW</h2>
<p>A functional ABW environment requires investment in a genuine range of settings at the right ratios for the workforce. Research from Leesman (the largest global database of workplace experience) suggests: focus settings (individual enclosed or semi-enclosed) at approximately 25–30% of the floor plate; collaboration settings at 35–40%; social and informal settings at 15–20%; and meeting rooms at 15–20%. The mix should be calibrated to actual work pattern data, not industry averages.</p>

<h2>Change Management is Not Optional</h2>
<p>The most consistent predictor of ABW success is not the quality of the furniture or the design of the floor plate — it is the quality of the change management process. Workers who understand why the change is happening, who have been involved in the design of their new environment, and who have been trained in how to use the new settings adapt successfully. Workers who arrive to find their desk gone without explanation or support will not adapt, regardless of how good the design is.</p>

<h2>The Hybrid Work Modification</h2>
<p>Post-2022, successful ABW environments in Australia have evolved to incorporate team neighbourhoods — areas of the floor plate with a loose association with specific teams, even without assigned desks. This modification addresses the most common complaint about pure ABW: the loss of social cohesion and team identity. A neighbourhood has a base that the team can customise with whiteboards, team materials, and visual identity, while the broader floor plate provides access to the full range of settings.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Plan Your ABW Environment</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk works with organisations planning activity-based workplaces. Our free office layout planning service can help you design a setting mix that works for your team.</p>
</div>
`,
