    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Workplace Strategy Consultation", href: "/workplace-strategy" }, { anchor: "Free Office Layout Plan", href: "/free-office-layout-plan" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">The premise of hybrid work office design is often misunderstood as: "fewer people, so we need less space." This leads to undersized offices with insufficient settings, reduced investment in quality, and an environment that validates the preference for working from home rather than challenging it. Designing for hybrid work correctly means designing an office that offers what home genuinely cannot — not a smaller version of what was there before, but a meaningfully better experience for the activities the office is best suited to.</p>

<h2>What the Home Office Does Well</h2>
<p>Understanding the home office's genuine strengths is essential for designing a competitive alternative. Home offices deliver: uninterrupted focus work (no open-plan noise), flexible working hours, elimination of commute time, personal comfort customisation, and proximity to domestic responsibilities. The office cannot win on all these dimensions. It should not try. The design focus should be on the dimensions where the office has genuine advantages.</p>

<h2>What the Office Does Better</h2>
<p>The office has genuine advantages in: spontaneous social interaction and relationship building; complex multi-person collaboration requiring physical presence; access to specialised infrastructure (AV systems, prototyping equipment, large-format printing); onboarding and mentoring of new team members; access to senior leaders and informal hierarchy navigation; and the psychological separation between work and home life that some workers (particularly those in shared accommodation) cannot achieve at home. The hybrid office design should maximise and make visible these advantages.</p>

<h2>Collaboration-First Planning</h2>
<p>The practical implication of hybrid work design principles is a significant shift in space allocation away from individual workstations and toward collaboration infrastructure. A 2023 CBRE analysis of post-pandemic fitouts in Australian CBD buildings found that the ratio of collaboration to focus space in new fitouts has shifted from roughly 30:70 to 50:50 over the period 2020–2023, with some organisations going further toward 60% collaboration settings. This is not a permanent equilibrium — most designers expect some rebalancing toward focus as attendance patterns stabilise.</p>

<h2>Technology Integration Requirements</h2>
<p>Hybrid work creates a specific technology requirement that older office environments cannot satisfy: every meeting room and collaboration zone must support remote participation as a first-class experience, not an afterthought. This means wide-angle cameras that capture all participants in the room, directional microphones that transmit clearly without background noise, displays that show remote participants at life-size or close to it, and simple connectivity from any device without a technician. Meeting rooms without this capability are functionally broken in a hybrid context.</p>

<h2>Neighbourhood Design for Team Cohesion</h2>
<p>The most effective structural response to hybrid attendance patterns is the team neighbourhood: a defined zone of the floor plate associated with a specific team, with a mix of bench seating, meeting points, whiteboards, and informal gathering spaces. Neighbourhoods give teams a home base that provides social cohesion without assigned desks. They can be customised with team visual identity, project materials, and the specific collaboration tools (physical or digital) that the team uses. Most importantly, they give remote workers a visible place to land when they come in, reducing the disorientation of arriving in an undifferentiated space.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design a Hybrid-Ready Workplace</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk helps organisations design offices that perform in a hybrid work context. Request a free layout planning consultation today.</p>
</div>
`,
