    content: `
<p class="lead">Workplace wellness has evolved from a HR programme (fruit bowls, yoga classes) to a design specification. The organisations building the most competitive and sought-after workplaces in Australia are embedding health and wellbeing into the physical environment at a structural level — through ergonomic furniture specification, lighting design, air quality management, acoustic treatment, access to nature, and dedicated spaces for mental health and physical recovery. This is not altruism: the evidence connecting workplace physical quality to employee health, retention, and productivity is now substantial enough to make wellness design a straightforward investment decision.</p>

<h2>Ergonomic Furniture as a Duty of Care</h2>
<p>Ergonomic furniture — sit-stand desks, AFRDI-certified task chairs, monitor arms, keyboard trays, and anti-fatigue matting — is the foundational wellness investment. Musculoskeletal disorders are the most common work-related health condition in Australia, responsible for more lost workdays than any other cause. The connection between poor ergonomic furniture specification and musculoskeletal injury rates is direct and well-established. Organisations that continue to specify non-ergonomic furniture are accepting a measurable cost in injury claims, absenteeism, and reduced productivity.</p>

<h2>Lighting for Health</h2>
<p>Lighting quality has profound effects on wellbeing, fatigue, and circadian rhythm regulation. The specification priorities for wellness-oriented office lighting: colour temperature that varies across the day (warmer in morning and late afternoon, cooler at midday) to support circadian alignment; minimum 300 lux on the work surface with 500+ lux in focus areas; access to daylight or daylight-mimicking light sources; elimination of flicker (a significant source of eye strain and headaches); and glare control from both natural and artificial sources.</p>

<h2>Air Quality and Ventilation</h2>
<p>Indoor air quality is one of the most consistently underspecified elements of commercial fitout design. CO2 concentration above 1000ppm — common in enclosed meeting rooms — is associated with measurable cognitive impairment. Poor air quality is implicated in headaches, fatigue, and respiratory irritation. Wellness-focused fitouts specify CO2 monitoring in enclosed spaces, MERV 13+ filtration in HVAC systems, low-VOC materials and finishes, and ventilation rates that exceed the minimum code requirements.</p>

<h2>Mental Health Spaces</h2>
<p>The explicit provision of quiet spaces, reflection rooms, and decompression zones within commercial offices is a relatively recent development that is becoming increasingly standard in premium Australian fitouts. These spaces serve workers who need a moment of genuine quiet — for sensory decompression, prayer, meditation, or simply recovery from an intense meeting. They are typically small, simply furnished with comfortable seating and soft lighting, acoustically isolated, and bookable for privacy. Their presence signals organisational awareness of mental health needs in a way that words in an employee handbook cannot.</p>

<h2>Movement Infrastructure</h2>
<p>Wellness-focused offices design for movement throughout the workday, not just in dedicated gym facilities. This means: locating coffee and collaboration points at distances from work areas that encourage walking; providing end-of-trip facilities (showers, lockers, bike storage) that enable active commuting; designing circulation routes that pass through green or outdoor spaces; and providing standing and walking meeting opportunities through high-table settings and outdoor terraces. The physical environment either supports or inhibits movement — the wellness-focused fitout systematically removes friction from healthy movement choices.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Build a Wellness-Focused Workplace</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk helps Australian organisations specify furniture and environments that genuinely support employee health. Request a consultation today.</p>
</div>
`,
