    readTime: "5 min read",
    publishDate: "2025-12-25",
    excerpt: "The greige era of Australian commercial interiors — grey, white, and beige as the universal safe choices — is ending. The palette of premium and contemporary offices has expanded dramatically toward earthy tones, saturated hues, and considered colour strategies grounded in psychology and brand.",
    tags: ["colour", "design", "trends", "interiors"],
    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Case Studies", href: "/case-studies" }, { anchor: "Workplace Strategy", href: "/workplace-strategy" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">The greige era of Australian commercial interiors — the decade in which grey, white, and beige were universal safe choices that communicated neither success nor failure, neither care nor neglect — is ending. The palette of premium and contemporary Australian offices has expanded dramatically toward earthy tones, saturated hues, deep accent colours, and considered colour strategies that are grounded in colour psychology, brand alignment, and the genuine desire to create environments that feel different from a generic office.</p>

<h2>The Science of Colour in Work Environments</h2>
<p>Colour psychology research provides a framework for commercial colour decision-making. Key findings relevant to workplace design: blue tones increase focus and are associated with trustworthiness and calm — appropriate for financial, legal, and analytical work environments; green tones reduce eye strain and create a sense of balance and refreshment — widely used in healthcare and wellness-oriented environments; warm tones (terracotta, amber, rust) increase energy and social interaction — most effective in collaboration and social zones; red in high concentration increases heart rate and can increase stress — most appropriate as a limited accent. Grey and white are not neutral — they reduce energy and can increase depression risk in high concentrations in low-light environments.</p>

<h2>Earthy and Organic Tones</h2>
<p>The dominant colour movement in Australian commercial interiors in 2025 is the earthy palette: terracotta, sage green, warm ochre, burnt sienna, clay, and camel. These tones connect to the Australian natural landscape, work well with timber and stone materials, and feel neither corporate-generic nor aggressively fashion-forward. They have sufficient warmth to prevent the coldness of grey environments while being sophisticated enough for professional contexts. They photograph well, which matters in an era where workplaces are marketed on social media.</p>

<h2>Brand Colour Integration</h2>
<p>Forward-thinking organisations are using the physical office environment to reinforce brand colour systems rather than choosing generic commercial palettes. This is not the crude approach of painting all walls in the brand's primary colour — it is the considered integration of brand-adjacent tones, materials, and graphic elements that create a coherent visual identity between the digital brand and the physical workspace. The approach requires collaboration between the brand team and the interior designer and is most effective in customer-facing spaces (reception, client meeting rooms) where brand consistency matters most.</p>

<h2>Zone-Based Colour Strategy</h2>
<p>The most sophisticated approach to office colour in 2025 is zone-based colour: different colour palettes for different functional zones, creating wayfinding, hierarchy, and experiential differentiation simultaneously. Focus zones in cool, calming tones; collaboration zones in warmer, energising tones; social zones in the most expressive and inviting palette; executive zones in the most restrained and authoritative colours. This approach replaces the bland uniformity of a single commercial colour choice with a thoughtful spatial narrative.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Plan Your Office Colour Strategy</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk helps organisations choose furniture finishes and materials that work with considered colour strategies. Contact our team for guidance.</p>
</div>
`,
